UNIT carried_canonical
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@b15b56613f9d cli/sir/v05.mjs canonical — the form every CARRIED CANONICAL-SHA256 is computed over and every
  language's bound table must reproduce; reproduce the code exactly
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG carried_canonical(table: map[text, json]) -> text
ENVELOPE table is a map from text to json, at any depth. Every key and every text value is well-formed (no unpaired
  surrogate). Every key spelling is an ordinary key, including __proto__, the index-like spellings and the codec-marker
  spellings __t and __throw. Numbers that are NaN, infinite or negative zero are IN as throw observations (R6). A non-map
  table and host-only values are OUT; the js file widens to them (OUT-OF-DOMAIN).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Render a carried constant table as one compact JSON text with the keys of every map, at every depth, in
  KEY ORDER, so the same table hashes alike however it was written down, and refuse any number JSON cannot tell apart.
  The text is what a CARRIED CANONICAL-SHA256 is the SHA-256 of (over its bytes); hashing is not part of this unit.
BEHAVIOR
R1 The result is compact JSON: no whitespace anywhere outside text values. A map is an opening brace, its entries as
  key-text, a colon and the rendered value, separated by commas, and a closing brace; an empty map is the two braces
  alone. A list is its elements rendered in their own order between square brackets, separated by commas. true, false and
  none render as "true", "false" and "null".
R2 KEY ORDER, applied to the entries of EVERY map, the table itself and every map nested at any depth (directly or
  inside a list): ascending order of each key's sequence of 16-bit code units, compared unit by unit, a proper prefix
  first. A character beyond the basic multilingual plane counts as its surrogate pair, so it sorts before the private-use
  and compatibility characters at the top of the basic plane. No key is special: digit-only keys sort as text ("10"
  precedes "9"). The order the entries arrive in never matters.
R3 A list keeps its elements in their own order; only map entries are reordered.
R4 A text, key or value, renders between double quotes with exactly these escapes: quotation mark and backslash are
  preceded by a backslash; backspace, form feed, line feed, carriage return and tab become backslash-b, -f, -n, -r, -t;
  every other control character (code 0 through 31) becomes backslash-u and four LOWERCASE hex digits. Everything else is
  written raw: solidus, delete, the line and paragraph separators, and every non-ASCII character. Keys sort (R2) on their
  raw text, never on the escaped form.
R5 Positive zero renders "0". Any other finite number renders by the ECMAScript Number-to-String algorithm. Take the
  shortest decimal digit string d (k digits, no leading or trailing zero) and the integer n such that the magnitude is d times ten to the power n - k
  and that round-trips to the same double (the closest to the exact value when several are shortest). Then: when
  k <= n <= 21, write d followed by n - k zeros; when 0 < n <= 21, write the first n digits, a point, and the rest; when
  -6 < n <= 0, write "0.", then -n zeros, then d; otherwise write the first digit, a point and the remaining digits when
  k > 1, then "e", the sign of n - 1 ("+" or "-") and its magnitude. A negative number carries a leading "-". So 255 is
  "255", 1e20 is "100000000000000000000", 1e21 is "1e+21", 0.000001 is "0.000001" and 1e-7 is "1e-7".
R6 A number that is NaN, +Infinity, -Infinity or negative zero, anywhere in the table, makes the whole call throw;
  nothing is rendered CITED FIDELITY package — JSON has no form that tells these apart from zero or none
INVARIANT the result depends only on the table's content, never on the order entries arrive in, at any depth.
INVARIANT on success the result parses as JSON and denotes the same table; distinct tables give distinct results.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN (the js file states these as its widening) a table that is not a map (none, text, a number, a bool, a list,
  a host mapping object) throws. Anywhere inside, a valueless entry or element, a list hole, an arbitrary-precision
  integer, a boxed scalar, a pattern object, a set, a byte array, or any other object that is not a plain map or list
  throws. An unpaired surrogate in a key or text does not throw: it renders as backslash-u and four lowercase hex digits.
ORACLE value -> oracles/carried_canonical.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 ce67efdc6101b1a2c73e1180fec6ecd759e9cbc63bdc92eed32bc68985e2a87b
SCOPE IN: R1–R6 over map[text, json] tables: key order at every depth, index-like and reserved-looking keys, every
  escape class, the number layout boundaries (1e21, 1e-7, 0.000001, subnormals, the largest double), and the NaN,
  infinity and negative-zero throws. OUT: host-only values (js widening); hashing (checkCarried) and table reading
  (carriedTable, rustCarriedTable) are other units.
PROVENANCE rederive@b15b56613f9d (MIT), cli/sir/v05.mjs `canonical` (with `plainMap`); reached through
  selfhost/seams/carried_canonical.mjs. Cut 2026-10-26; round 2 re-cut against the fixed kernel (round 1 was b329a40412df,
  which sorted only the top level, hoisted index keys and rendered NaN, infinity and negative zero); every rule probed
  against the real function.
